<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/expert/geophysics-of-plate-driving-forces">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Plate tectonics is confirmed geology — but <em>confirmed</em> means the boundaries,
    the rates and the record, and the remaining hard problem is the mechanics: how the
    Earth's heat engine actually converts internal energy into moving plates. The
    driving-force question generates papers, geoneutrino measurements and tomographic
    imaging campaigns sixty years after the theory's acceptance, because the forces
    are near-invisible and the candidates — convection drag, slab pull, ridge push —
    compete within the same factor of a few. This page walks the syllabus's plate
    tectonics topics (Wegener's drift, the Earth's layers, magmatism, the boundary
    types, hot spots) each weighted for the question they answer about the engine.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the power budget, the convection reality, the force balance, open problems" widget="equationStep" config={driverCfg} />

  <h2>Wegener's arguments and the missing engine</h2>
  <p>
    Alfred Wegener (1912) assembled continental drift's evidence (2.1): the jigsaw fit
    of the Atlantic margins, matching fossils (Mesosaurus, Glossopteris) across
    now-distant shores, matching mountain belts (the Appalachians' continuation in
    Scotland's Caledonides), and ancient glacial deposits straddling the tropics —
    explicable only if the continents had moved through the latitudes. His theory was
    rejected, correctly on its own terms: he proposed continents ploughing through
    oceanic crust, and no known force could do that, so the evidence floated without
    a mechanism. The rescue came from the seafloor: the 1950s–60s mapping of
    mid-ocean ridges, their symmetrical magnetic stripes (Vine–Matthews, 1963), and
    the youthful age of ocean crust (nowhere older than about 200 million years,
    versus 4-billion-year continents) established seafloor spreading — and inverted
    Wegener's geometry: <em>the plates move, and the continents are passengers</em>.
    The expert lesson is methodological: a theory with overwhelming evidence and no
    mechanism is a hypothesis with a vacancy sign, and the vacancy was filled by
    mantle physics — which is why the driving-force question, still partially open,
    is the living edge of the same story.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "continents plough through the ocean floor like
    ships through water." Wegener's version failed exactly here, and the modern
    picture inverts it: oceanic crust is the <em>top of the moving plate</em>, and it
    is recycled — created at ridges, destroyed at trenches — while the continents are
    the low-density, buoyant part that cannot subduct and merely rides along. The
    ocean floor is younger than many mountain belts precisely because it is
    conveyor-belt material, not the sea the ships sail on.
  </p>

  <h2>Layers of the Earth: rheology is the real division</h2>
  <p>
    The layered Earth (2.2) is usually drawn as crust, mantle, core — a chemical
    division. The geophysical one that matters for plate driving is
    <strong>rheological</strong>: how each layer deforms. The <strong>lithosphere</strong>
    (crust plus the mantle's stiff lid) behaves elastically over million-year
    timescales and breaks — it is the plates, typically 100 km thick under oceans.
    The <strong>asthenosphere</strong> beneath it (roughly 100–350 km depth) is hot
    enough, close to its melting curve, to flow — slowly, solid rock creeping like
    glacier ice, enabled by dislocation creep at atomic scale. That solid-yet-flowing
    layer is what makes plate motion mechanically possible: low resistance beneath,
    rigid rafts above. Below, mantle viscosity climbs with pressure; the D″ layer
    at the core–mantle boundary (~2,900 km down) hosts two giant slow provinces
    (beneath Africa and the Pacific) whose role in the engine is active research.
    The energy budget is measured two ways: surface heat flow (about 47 terawatts
    escaping) and geoneutrinos — antineutrinos from uranium and thorium decay,
    detected by KamLAND (2005) and Borexino, which confirmed the radiogenic share
    (roughly half) directly. Half the engine's fuel is primordial heat, half is a
    nuclear reactor the size of the planet.
  </p>

  <h2>Magmatism at the boundaries: where the melt comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Magmatism (2.3) is the engine's exhaust and its diagnostic. The physics:
    mantle rock melts not primarily by heating but by <strong>decompression</strong> —
    hot rock rises, the pressure that kept it solid drops, and it melts (adiabatic
    decompression) — and by <strong>fluxing</strong>: water released from a subducting
    slab lowers the overlying mantle's melting point. The three plate settings each
    produce characteristic melt. Divergent ridges: decompression melting as mantle
    rises beneath the spreading axis, generating the basalt that floors every ocean
    (mid-ocean ridge basalt, remarkably uniform). Convergent zones: the wet flux
    melts the mantle wedge above the slab, and the water-rich magma evolves into
    andesite and rhyolite — the explosive arc volcanoes (the Andes, Cascades,
    Indonesia), because dissolved water makes eruptions gas-driven. Hot spots:
    plume melting away from boundaries — Hawaii's shield volcanoes. The geochemistry
    is a fingerprint reader: arc lavas are water-rich and silica-evolved; ridge
    lavas are dry and primitive; plume lavas carry trace-element signatures of deep
    reservoirs. Reading magma chemistry is reading the engine's plumbing diagram.
  </p>

  <h2>Divergent, convergent and transform: the force balance in action</h2>
  <p>
    The boundary types (2.4–2.7) are where the driving forces are measured.
    <strong>Slab pull</strong>: a subducting plate is cold — about 100 °C colder and
    correspondingly denser than the mantle it sinks into — so the slab hangs and pulls
    the trailing plate like a weight on a rope. Forsyth and Uyeda's classic 1975 force
    analysis found plate velocities correlate strongly with the fraction of their edge
    that is subducting: slab pull dominates, plausibly contributing of order 10¹³
    newtons per metre of trench. <strong>Ridge push</strong>: new crust is created
    elevated at the ridge and cools, thickens and sinks as it spreads — the oceanic
    plate slides down a slope it itself builds; a real but secondary force (roughly
    a tenth of slab pull). <strong>Basal drag</strong>: mantle flow traction on plate
    bottoms — locally important, globally contested in sign (some plates are dragged
    forward, some held back). The confirmation is quantitative: GPS geodesy measures
    present-day plate motion to millimetres per year (the Pacific plate's interior
    moves about 7–10 cm/yr, the Atlantic opening at 2–4), matching the
    million-year record from magnetic stripes — two clocks agreeing is the
    strongest confirmation geophysics offers. The classic school picture — plates
    riding convection cells like luggage on a conveyor — is now known to be
    inverted: <em>plates mostly drive themselves, and the mantle follows</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the plates float on a sea of molten rock." The
    mantle is overwhelmingly solid: seismology shows it transmits shear waves, which
    fluids cannot carry, and the asthenosphere is perhaps 0.1–1% melted at most. What
    it does is <em>flow as a solid</em> — centimetres per year, like glacier ice, by
    atomic-scale creep under sustained stress. The distinction is not pedantry: the
    whole force balance (slabs sinking through solid mantle, plumes rising through
    it) happens in solid rock, and "molten layer" pictures make the physics
    impossible to reason about.
  </p>

  <h2>Hot spots and mantle plumes: the engine's fixed points</h2>
  <p>
    Hot spots (2.8) are volcanism away from boundaries — Hawaii, Iceland, Yellowstone —
    explained since Morgan (1971) as <strong>mantle plumes</strong>: narrow columns of
    buoyant hot rock rising, perhaps, from the core–mantle boundary, melting by
    decompression near the surface. The evidence is the geometry: a moving plate
    over a (near-)fixed plume draws a <em>hotspot track</em> — the Hawaiian
    emperor chain, volcanoes ageing linearly to the northwest, each dated,
    together a 60-million-year record of Pacific plate motion (with a famous bend
    47 million years ago recording a change in plate direction). Hot spots are
    also how the debate over the mantle's plumbing stays alive: whether plumes
    rise whole from the core–mantle boundary or form only in the upper mantle is
    contested (seismic tomography images fast conduits under some hot spots and
    not others), and whether "fixed" is fair — hot spots drift relative to each
    other — is measured, not assumed. The plume debate matters beyond Hawaii:
    plumes are the engine's second mode (independent of plates), linked to
    large igneous provinces whose eruptions (Siberian Traps, 252 million years
    ago) coincide with the greatest mass extinctions — the engine's
    occasional catastrophes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "ridges are where the mantle is hottest, so the
    plates rise there like boiling soup." Ridge mantle is only modestly warm — its
    topography is explained by <em>thermal contraction of the plate itself</em>: young
    oceanic lithosphere is hot, thin and buoyant (hence high), and it sinks about
    3 km as it ages, thickens and cools — which is exactly the "ridge push" slope
    from the force balance. The ridges mark where plates <em>separate</em>, letting
    normal-temperature mantle rise and melt by decompression — a consequence of
    the motion, not its engine.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>How the forces are actually measured.</strong> The force balance is
    inferred three ways. <em>Global force modelling</em>: Forsyth and Uyeda's
    approach — solve the inverse problem from observed plate velocities, which
    revealed the slab-pull dominance. <em>Seismic focal mechanisms</em>: the
    orientation and depth of earthquakes within slabs records the stress state —
    down-dip tension in the upper slab (pulled), compression deeper (resisted) —
    direct stress readings from the planet. <em>Tomography</em>: seismic waves
    speed through cold slab rock, so slabs can be imaged — many traced to the
    core–mantle boundary (the Farallon slab beneath the Americas), confirming
    that subduction communicates with the deepest mantle — evidence for
    whole-mantle convection against the older layered-mantle hypothesis. The
    remaining unknowns are the honest open set: the exact viscosity structure
    (which sets how much slab pull the mantle resists), the role of the two
    deep slow provinces, and why Earth runs plate tectonics at all while
    Venus — same size, same heat — does not (the planetary-geology page's
    comparative question).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Supercontinent cycles and the long-term engine.</strong> The
    plate system oscillates on half-billion-year rhythms: continents gather
    (Pangaea, 335–175 million years ago; Rodinia before it) and disperse,
    with Wilson cycles (ocean opens, widens, closes by subduction, continents
    collide) as the unit. The engine's long-term behaviour feeds climate and
    life: seafloor spreading rates modulate CO₂ outgassing; continental
    configuration moves weathering (the carbon–silicate thermostat) and
    ocean-circulation gateways (the Drake Passage's opening helped build the
    Antarctic Circumpolar Current and Antarctic glaciation). Numerical
    geodynamics — codes like StagYY and ASPECT simulating billions of years of
    convection on supercomputers — now reproduce supercontinent assembly and
    dispersal and test which parameter sets (viscosity, rayleigh number,
    yield strength) produce plate-tectonic behaviour at all. The driving-force
    question and the habitability question turn out to be the same question at
    different zoom levels — which is why this "abstract mechanics" topic sits
    at the centre of modern Earth science.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why tectonics may be a habitability ingredient.</strong> The
    comparative argument: Earth's carbon–silicate cycle (volcanism emitting
    CO₂, mountain-building and rainfall weathering it away) has stabilised
    climate over geological time — a feedback that needs plates to recycle
    crust. Venus, without plate tectonics, kept its carbon in the atmosphere
    (runaway greenhouse, 460 °C); Mars, small and frozen, lost its engine
    early. A planet with liquid water, continents and active tectonics may
    be the long-habitable type — an inference astrobiology now builds
    detection strategies on (the next page's subject). The driving forces,
    in other words, are not just why the continents move: they are part of
    why the surface has stayed livable for four billion years.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Forsyth & Uyeda (1975) force analysis; KamLAND geoneutrino results (2005); van der Hilst tomography reviews; Tackley computational geodynamics.</p>
</TopicPage>
